<Item XName="Note" UserName="Note" Type="Notes">
<Selected><T>Bool</T><D>0</D></Selected>
<Text>&lt;P class=MsoTitle style=&quot;BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 0.75pt solid; PADDING-BOTTOM: 1pt; PADDING-TOP: 0in; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT size=5 face=Arial&gt;Interference IM3 vs Beam Scanning&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;FONT size=3&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;Keysight Technologies SystemVue Workspace&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = &quot;o&quot; ns = &quot;urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office&quot; /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;FONT size=3&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;This workspace shows the 3rd-order intermodulation power versus receiver beam scanning angle.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;When there is an interference at a frequency different than the target signal, amplifier nonlinearity introduces nonlinear intermodulation products. The intermods possess certain phase characteristic. When scanning the receiver beam, the intermod power at the output of the Rx beamformer changes with the beam scan angle. The effect appears like a signal coming in at a frequency (intermod frequency) from a particular angle and&amp;nbsp;could be misinterpreted as a another receiving signal that does not exist in reality.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Rx Power vs Scan Angle&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;In Design2 schematic, Signal1 is a tone at 1GHz and is incident to the 8x8 receiver at (theta = thetaSig1, phi = phiSig1) degree. Signal2 is a tone at 900MHz and is incident to the 8x8 receiver at (theta = thetaSig2, phi = phiSig2) degree. The Rx uses RFLink_PhasedArray with Design1, which is a typical phase-shift based RF&amp;nbsp;beamforming design with&amp;nbsp;nonlinear amplifier.&amp;nbsp;The Rx beam is scanned across theta = [0:90] degree and phi = [0:360] degree. Within the simulation bandwidth, we can observe two third-order intermodulation (IM3), one&amp;nbsp;is located at 2*F1 - F2 = 1100Mhz and the other one is located at 2*F2 - F1 = 800Mhz. The MATLAB Script model M1 in Design2 schematic computes Rx power at the two signal frequencies 1000MHz and 900MHz, and also at the two IMS frequencies 800MHz and 1100MHz.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Run DF2 first and then run &quot;PlotRxPowerVsScanAngle&quot; equation. Rx power vs scan angle at 800MHz, 900MHz, 1000MHz,&amp;nbsp;and 1100MHz will be shown in 3D graphs. The unit is in dBm. &lt;FONT size=2&gt;The plot at the intermod frequency,&amp;nbsp;(2*F1 - F2)&amp;nbsp;and (2*F2 - F1), looks like a signal coming in at&amp;nbsp;a&amp;nbsp;specific&amp;nbsp;angle, but there is no such signal, this is just the result of nonlinear intermodulation.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Rx Spectrum vs Tunable Scan Angle&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;You can turn on ContinuousMode of the SpectraumAnalyzer in Design3, run DF3, and tune rxScanTheta, rxScanPhi and observe the Rx output&amp;nbsp;spectrum of Signal1, Signal2, and the two intermods.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;</Text>
